</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">PVLDB Artifact Availability:
<br class="ltx_break"></span><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The source code, data, and/or other artifacts have been made available at </span><a href="https://URL_TO_YOUR_ARTIFACTS" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">URL_TO_YOUR_ARTIFACTS</a><span id="p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span><span id="p2.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Machine learning over relations was traditionally done through a reduction to learning over unstructured data: flatten the data into a <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">feature matrix</em>, with a collection of predefined queries that produce features for every prediction instance, and then learn a flat model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perlich and Provost, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kimelfeld and Ré, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakshi et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Progress in artificial neural networks, and specifically Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and graph transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al., 2024</a>)</cite> changed this practice: the best performing models now deploy prediction models directly from the structured data without ad-hoc feature extraction<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, deep learning is commonly applied to relational databases by translating them into a graph representation:
nodes represent database entities (usually tuples),
and edges represent relationships (usually foreign-primary key constraints).
GNNs or graph transformers are then trained on these graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fey et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cappuzzo et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tönshoff et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlichtkrull and Søgaard, 2017</a>)</cite>.
To handle the varying <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">types</em> of nodes and edges determined by the database schema,
<em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heterogeneous</em> graph models are used <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
This creates a two-paradigm pipeline: the relational database defines the graph structure,
while the (graph-based) machine learning library learns the model.
At inference time, new data is exported to the graph representation, predictions are computed from the learned model, and results are reintegrated into the database.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09482v1/Fig_intro3.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:404/104;" width="404" height="104" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An <em id="S1.F1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explanation view</em> defines a subdatabase (or more generally, a derived view).
The rest of the database gets randomly perturbed. As long as the view is kept the same,
a trained black-box model gives similar predictions over the perturbed database as the original database.
We say that the view <em id="S1.F1.6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft determines</em> the prediction.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The neural network model induced by the above graph-learning approach over the database can be viewed as a complex numerical function constructed from the database’s tuples and values, and parameterized by a large number of learned weights.
Consequently, as in other modalities of deep learning, the model’s internal behavior is inherently opaque and cannot be readily understood through direct inspection. To address this, the field of <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI)</em> provides tools that shed light on different aspects of the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelov et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arenas, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marques-Silva, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Darwiche, 2023</a>)</cite>. These include <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local explanations</em>, which account for the model’s output on a specific instance, and <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">global explanations</em>, which aim to characterize the model’s behavior as a whole.
Explanations may be <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contrastive</em> (sometimes called <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">counterfactual</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barceló et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), identifying hypothetical changes that would alter the model’s outcome, or <em id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">abductive</em>, pointing to focused parts of the input that suffice to determine the outcome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marques-Silva, 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we study global abductive explanations for deep relational models. Our focus is on settings where the underlying schema consists of multiple interrelated tables with numerous attributes—a common scenario in real-world databases.
We aim to identify concise, informative subsets of the data that best account for the model’s predictions.
Specifically, we address the question: <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Which parts of the database does the model rely on most when making predictions?</em>
Hence, our goal is to provide global insights into the model’s behavior by uncovering the database fragments that are most influential across predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Example 1.</h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To illustrate the nature of our explanations, consider the
“rel-trial” database <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Initiative et al., 2019</a>)</cite> from the RelBench collection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robinson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Suppose we wish to explain a binary classifier that solves the following task: “Predict whether a trial will achieve its primary outcome.” Here, a trial
corresponds to a record in the relation </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Studies</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Our approach identifies only <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 10\%</annotation></semantics></math> out of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="140" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>140</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">140</annotation></semantics></math> columns together with the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> relevant primary-foreign key pairs, which capture the schema components that largely determine the model’s output.
In particular, our explanation highlights the attributes </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">name</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">city</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">country</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> from the </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Facilities</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> relation, which is linked to </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Studies</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> via the intermediate relation </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Facilities_studies</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. This indicates that the names and locations of the utilized facilities highly influence the success or failure of the study. Interestingly, no attributes from the </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Outcomes</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> relation, e.g., the outcome type or description, are included in the explanation, indicating that they are not actually as useful for the model to predict whether this outcome is achieved.
Our explanations further show that specific study designs (i.e., records in the </span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Designs</span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> relation) that utilize simple interventions are important for the model’s prediction. A detailed case study follows in <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3. Case Study ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>.
To verify that the schema components identified by our explanation capture the most important parts of the database, we train a smaller model using only those components, which achieves similar performance (ROC_AUC drops only slightly from <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="71.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>71.28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">71.28</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="69.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>69.78</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">69.78</annotation></semantics></math>). This is still high performance for the task (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robinson et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for reference), while reducing the database by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 80\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To establish insights such as those in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Example</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we propose a general framework in which explanations are expressed as <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">view definitions</em> written in SQL (or any other query language of choice).
This grounds explanations in a representation that is familiar to database users, in contrast to common alternatives that require machine learning expertise to interpret <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Räuker et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For graph-based models, this would require knowledge of the graph modeling details or even the inner workings of GNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To this aim, we deploy the classical notion of <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">determinacy</em>, introduced by Nash, Segoufin, and Vianu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nash et al., 2010</a>)</cite>, that captures in a fundamental way when a collection of database views is enough to determine the result of a query. For reasons of usability, flexibility, and practical implementation, we propose a soft and statistical adaptation of this concept.
Roughly speaking, the definition we propose identifies an explanation as <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">accurate</em> if the model is likely to have <em id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">similar predictions</em> on our database as it would have on alternative databases, as long as they agree on the views. <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows a visual illustration of the concept.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to accuracy, we seek <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concise</em> explanations that are easy to digest. Clearly, the verbosity of an explanation depends on both the number of views and the complexity of each view. Our framework naturally supports the tradeoff between accuracy and conciseness, allowing users to balance interpretability with fidelity to the model’s behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, the theoretical definition that we propose for explanations does not lend itself directly to an implementation.
We develop heuristic techniques for the case of GNNs and important classes of views: projections, joins over foreign keys, and selections (filters).
These heuristics are generally based on learning <em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masked</em> variations of the GNN at hand, where we place the masks in components of the GNN that correspond to the different kinds of views.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, we present an empirical evaluation of our techniques on the RelBench collection of databases and node-level tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robinson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In particular, the experiments show the tradeoff between the accuracy and conciseness of the explanations. As a side benefit, they also show that, for several of the RelBench tasks, we can achieve models of similar quality to the ones publicly available, while using only a fraction of the database.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our main contributions.</span>
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p10.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.p10.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
We introduce a model-agnostic framework for generating relational explanations of learned model behavior over relational databases, based on a statistical adaptation of determinacy. The framework uses relational queries, supports user-controlled granularity, and does not rely on internal model representations.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p10.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.p10.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
We instantiate the framework for relational deep learning using GNNs, and develop an efficient masking-based heuristic to generate explanations without explicitly estimating soft determinacy.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p10.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.p10.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
We conduct extensive experiments on the RelBench benchmark across several databases and tasks, proposing evaluation baselines and metrics. Our method consistently produces high-quality explanations at significantly lower computational cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we define the concepts and notation that we use throughout the paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Relational Database Concepts</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with database concepts and terminology.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Databases</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relation schema</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a sequence <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})=(A_{1},\dots,A_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})=(A_{1},\dots,A_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> of distinct <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attributes</em>, and a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">key signature</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})=(B_{1},\dots,B_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})=(B_{1},\dots,B_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math> is a subsequence of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each attribute <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a (finite or infinite) <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">domain</em>, denoted <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dom}(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dom}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{C}}=(C_{1},\dots,C_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{C}}=(C_{1},\dots,C_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a sequence of attributes of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, then we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dom}(\mathord{\mathbf{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dom}(\mathord{\mathbf{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dom}(C_{1})\times\dots\times\mathsf{dom}(C_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dom}(C_{1})\times\dots\times\mathsf{dom}(C_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tuple</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> over a relation schema <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a member of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dom}(\mathsf{att}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dom}(\mathsf{att}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t[A_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t[A_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th value in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to the attribute <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>).
If <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{C}}=(C_{1},\dots,C_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{C}}=(C_{1},\dots,C_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of attributes from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, then we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]</annotation></semantics></math> the tuple
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t[C_{1}],\dots,t[C_{m}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t[C_{1}],\dots,t[C_{m}])</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relation</em> over the relation schema <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of tuples over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}[\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})]\neq t_{2}[\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}[\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})]\neq t_{2}[\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})]</annotation></semantics></math>
for every two distinct tuples <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}\neq t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\neq t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is a relation over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{C}}=(C_{1},\dots,C_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{C}}=(C_{1},\dots,C_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of attributes from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the relation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\{t[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\mid t\in r\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\{t[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\mid t\in r\}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|r|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|r|</annotation></semantics></math> the number of tuples in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">database schema</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a set <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> of <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relation names</em>, and it maps every relation name <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> to a relation schema <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To ease the notation, we may write <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> as a shorthand notation for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{S}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{S}(R))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{S}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{S}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
In addition, a database schema <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> includes a set <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FK}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FK}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> of <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">foreign-key constraints</em>, or <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">FK</em> for short, where an FK
is an expression of the form <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊑</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are relation names in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>),
the sequence <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of attributes from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequences <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> have the same length, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dom}(\mathord{\mathbf{C}})\subseteq\mathsf{dom}(\mathsf{key}(S))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dom}(\mathord{\mathbf{C}})\subseteq\mathsf{dom}(\mathsf{key}(S))</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">database</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> maps every relation name <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> to a relation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that every key constraint and every constraint in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FK}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FK}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied: the FK <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊑</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">satisfied</em> if
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\subseteq D(S)[\mathsf{key}(S)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\subseteq D(S)[\mathsf{key}(S)]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Relational queries and predictive models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a database schema. A <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">query</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a relation schema, which we consistently denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it maps every database <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to a relation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(D)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> is a relation name, then an <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model</em> is a query
(possibly very complicated and with learned parameters)
over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> whose purpose is to make a prediction (e.g., classification or regression) for every tuple in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, an <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model</em> (<em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">of dimension <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></em>) is a query <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">existence of prediction attribute <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></em>:
<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R}_{M})=(\mathsf{key}(R),A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R}_{M})=(\mathsf{key}(R),A)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an attribute with <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dom}(A)=\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dom}(A)=\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> stand for the set of real numbers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">key signature agreement</em>:
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R}_{M})=\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R}_{M})=\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instance-prediction relation correspondence</em>:
For every database <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(D)[\mathsf{key}(R)]=D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(D)[\mathsf{key}(R)]=D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
In words, if <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=M(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=M(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is the relation resulting from applying
<math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> has the exact same key values as the <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-relation of <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We refer to each tuple in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]</annotation></semantics></math>
(notice the projection on only the key attributes)
as an <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instance</em> (to be classified by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> a database, both over the schema <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> be an instance (i.e., a key tuple of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>) to which we apply the model
We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{D}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{D}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> the unique prediction of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the value <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t[A]\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t[A]\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of the unique tuple <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in M(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in M(D)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t[\mathsf{key}(R)]=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t[\mathsf{key}(R)]=s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Determinacy</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly recall the concept of <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">determinacy</em> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nash et al. (2010)</a></cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a schema, let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be queries over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, referred to as <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">views</em>, and let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be a query over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\{V_{1},\dots,V_{m}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\{V_{1},\dots,V_{m}\}}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">determines</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> if for all databases <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}(D_{1})=V_{i}(D_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}(D_{1})=V_{i}(D_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(D_{1})=Q(D_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(D_{1})=Q(D_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, knowing the result of every view suffices to determine the result of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> without even looking at the database itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Heterogeneous Graphs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we present the concepts and notation related to machine learning over heterogeneous graphs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hetero-graphs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heterogeneous featured graph</em>, or just <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hetero-graph</em> for short, is a quadruple <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> where:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of nodes;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of <em id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">typed directed edges</em>, that is, triples <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\lambda,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\lambda,v)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are nodes in <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">edge type</em>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">node-type function</em>, mapping every node <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> to a type <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(v)</annotation></semantics></math>; and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">node-feature function</em> that maps every node <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> to a vector <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\tau(v)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\tau(v)}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some predefined <em id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">feature dimension</em> <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\tau(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\tau(v)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each node type.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Every edge type <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">source type</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{src}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗌𝗋𝖼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{src}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and a <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target type</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{tgt}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗍𝗀𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{tgt}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we require all edges <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(u,\lambda,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(u,\lambda,v)</annotation></semantics></math> to be consistent in that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(u)={\mathsf{src}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗌𝗋𝖼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(u)={\mathsf{src}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(v)={\mathsf{tgt}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗍𝗀𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(v)={\mathsf{tgt}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(V)</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\{\tau(v)\mid v\in V\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\{\tau(v)\mid v\in V\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\tau(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\tau(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V[\alpha]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V[\alpha]</annotation></semantics></math> the set of nodes <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(v)=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(v)=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{in}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{in}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{out}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>𝗈𝗎𝗍</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{out}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>)
the set of incoming (resp., outgoing) neighbors of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the set <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\{u\mid\exists\lambda[(u,\lambda,v)\in E]\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\{u\mid\exists\lambda[(u,\lambda,v)\in E]\}}</annotation></semantics></math>
(resp., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\{u\mid\exists\lambda[(v,\lambda,u)\in E]\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\{u\mid\exists\lambda[(v,\lambda,u)\in E]\}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge type, then we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{in}}_{\lambda}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{in}}_{\lambda}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> the set of nodes <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{in}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{in}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(u,\lambda,v)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(u,\lambda,v)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>; analogously, we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{out}}_{\lambda}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝗈𝗎𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{out}}_{\lambda}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> the set of nodes <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{out}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>𝗈𝗎𝗍</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{N}^{\mathsf{out}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(v,\lambda,u)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(v,\lambda,u)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Node-centric models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a hetero-graph, and let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\tau(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\tau(V)</annotation></semantics></math> be a type, which we refer to as a <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prediction entity type</em>. A <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-model</em> (of dimension <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>)
is a function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:V[\alpha]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:V[\alpha]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, mapping every <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-node <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to a predicted vector <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the dimension <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the task (e.g., it is one in binary classification and regression, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-class classification), and is not necessarily the same as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Heterogeneous Graph Neural Networks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A GNN defines a model over a graph by applying message passing to produce a vector representation of each node; in each step, called <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">layer</em>, the vector of a node is updated by aggregating the vectors of its neighbors, referred to as <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">messages</em>.
A <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hetero-GNN</em> operates over a hetero-graph similarly, except that messages of each edge type are aggregated separately. We give a more precise definition of a typical hetero-GNN next. We note that our definition is slightly more general and detailed than common definitions (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barceló et al., 2022</a>)</cite>), stressing the role of labels and directionality in the GNN.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a hetero-graph and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\tau(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\tau(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A hetero-GNN with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers defines an <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-model using the functions <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0,\dots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0,\dots,L</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed inductively as follows.
First, define <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{0}(v)=\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{0}(v)=\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0,\dots,L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0,\dots,L-1</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{(\ell)}(v,\lambda,o)=\mathsf{AGG}(\mathord{\{\mskip-5.0mu\{\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}(u)\mid u\in\mathcal{N}^{o}_{\lambda}(v)\}\mskip-5.0mu\}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖠𝖦𝖦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.220em;" width="0.220em"><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{(\ell)}(v,\lambda,o)=\mathsf{AGG}(\mathord{\{\mskip-5.0mu\{\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}(u)\mid u\in\mathcal{N}^{o}_{\lambda}(v)\}\mskip-5.0mu\}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for every node <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, edge label <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and orientation <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in\mathord{\{{\mathsf{in}},{\mathsf{out}}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝗂𝗇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗈𝗎𝗍</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in\mathord{\{{\mathsf{in}},{\mathsf{out}}\}}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{o}_{\lambda}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{o}_{\lambda}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{AGG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖠𝖦𝖦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{AGG}</annotation></semantics></math> is an aggregation function such as average or max, and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\{\mskip-5.0mu\{\cdot\}\mskip-5.0mu\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mpadded style="width:0.220em;" width="0.220em"><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\{\mskip-5.0mu\{\cdot\}\mskip-5.0mu\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the bag (multiset) notation.
Next, for <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we define</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}(v)=\mathsf{UPD}(&amp;\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell-1)}(v),\\
&amp;\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{1},o_{1}),\dots,\\
&amp;\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{q},o_{q}))\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴𝖯𝖣</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}(v)=\mathsf{UPD}(&amp;\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell-1)}(v),\\
&amp;\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{1},o_{1}),\dots,\\
&amp;\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{q},o_{q}))\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">for some ordering <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{1},o_{1}),\dots,(\lambda_{q},o_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{1},o_{1}),\dots,(\lambda_{q},o_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> of all pairs <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,o)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{o}_{\lambda}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{o}_{\lambda}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined. Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{UPD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴𝖯𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{UPD}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">update function</em>, which is realized as a neural model. A typical example of model architecture for hetero-GNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlichtkrull et al., 2018</a>)</cite> involves a linear combination followed by an activation function (e.g., ReLU).
The model <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(v)=\mathsf{MLP}(\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(L)}(v))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖫𝖯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(v)=\mathsf{MLP}(\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(L)}(v))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MLP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬𝖫𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MLP}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multilayer-perception function that transforms the last layer into the actual prediction.
The learnable parameters are incorporated in the
functions <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{AGG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖠𝖦𝖦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{AGG}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{UPD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴𝖯𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{UPD}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MLP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MLP</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MLP}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Relational Learning via Heterogeneous Graphs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common way of applying deep learning over relational databases is to convert the database <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> into a hetero-graph <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then learn and apply a hetero-GNN over <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cvitkovic, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cappuzzo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fey et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Next, we describe this process more precisely for a typical conversion, known as the <em id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relational graph</em> due to the <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">r2n</span> (i.e., row-to-node) method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a schema, and let <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a database over <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The graph <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}=(V,E,\tau,\mathord{\mathbf{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows. The set <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of nodes consists of the pairs <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a relation name and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is tuple in <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathord{\{(R,t)\mid R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})\land t\in D(R)\}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathord{\{(R,t)\mid R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})\land t\in D(R)\}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For a node <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(v)=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(v)=R</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the type of a node is the name of its relation.
There is a directed edge <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(v,\varphi,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(v,\varphi,u)</annotation></semantics></math> from a node <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math> to a node <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(S,t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(S,t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> whenever the two are connected by a reference via an FK <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊑</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, the type of the edge is the FK that it corresponds to.
Finally, the feature vector <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows. Let <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a relation name with
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(R)=(A_{1},\dots,A_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(R)=(A_{1},\dots,A_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each attribute <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> we assume a trainable encoder
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A}:\mathsf{dom}(A)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{R.A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽𝗈𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A}:\mathsf{dom}(A)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{R.A}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for some predefined dimension <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{R.A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{R.A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, for each relation name <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> we assume a trainable encoder
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Enc}_{R}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{R_{\mathit{enc}}}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>𝑒𝑛𝑐</mi></msub></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Enc}_{R}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{R_{\mathit{enc}}}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for some dimension <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{R_{\mathit{enc}}}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}d_{R.A_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>𝑒𝑛𝑐</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{R_{\mathit{enc}}}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}d_{R.A_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a node <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math> we then
define:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}=\mathsf{Enc}_{R}\left(\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{1}}(t[A_{1}])\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}(t[A_{k}])\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Enc</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}=\mathsf{Enc}_{R}\left(\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{1}}(t[A_{1}])\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}(t[A_{k}])\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> stands for vector concatenation.
Notice that this formulation allows us to exclude some attributes <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of a relation <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> from the encoding, simply by setting <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Enc</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> to map every value to the empty vector. For example, in our implementation, we exclude key and foreign-key attributes from the features of a record.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, let <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a relation name in <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. An <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathcal{G}}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> over the database <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in a straightforward way: For a tuple <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(t[\mathsf{key}(R)])=M^{\prime}(v)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(t[\mathsf{key}(R)])=M^{\prime}(v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is the node <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math> in the node set <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V[R]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce our framework, where we leverage the concept of view determinacy to produce explanations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Explanation Views</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a schema with a relation name <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a database over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. We think of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a black-box complex model, involving a considerable number of computational components and learned parameters.
By an <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explanation</em> of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>’s behavior on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, we refer to a collection of views <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> (called <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explanation views</em>), each indicating a focused component of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that is important for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, we define the explanation views more formally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We assume an <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explanation language</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is simply a query language that we use as a formalism for defining explanation views.
An explanation view <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> states what component of the database <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> (existing or derived) is relevant for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to make the prediction <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{D}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{D}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> on any instance <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>; for that, we assume that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is parameterized by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This means that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> is an ordinary database query with the result <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, for every instance <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as a key tuple in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>;
hence, to represent explanation views, we assume that queries in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> can refer to the attributes of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> as constants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Example 1.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As an example, suppose that our schema consists of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. The following explanation view (in SQL) returns the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples that connect to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">create</span><span id="S3.Ex5.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> view <math id="S3.Ex5.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">select</span><span id="S3.Ex6.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S3.Ex6.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.Ex6.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">where</span><span id="S3.Ex7.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S3.Ex7.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T.A=s.A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T.A=s.A</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the parameterized view <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> does not necessarily have to refer to its parameter in its definition.
If <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> ignores its parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that it is <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instance-agnostic</em>, otherwise it is <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instance-specific</em>.
Formally, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is instance-agnostic if, for each database <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and instances <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D)=V\mathord{\langle s^{\prime}\rangle}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D)=V\mathord{\langle s^{\prime}\rangle}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As an example, the following explanation view is instance-agnostic, as it refers to all tuples of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of their relationship to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">select</span><span id="S3.Ex8.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S3.Ex8.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.Ex8.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, by an <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explanation</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> we mean a finite subset of concrete queries from <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quality of an explanation is measured using two criteria: <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">determinacy</em> (i.e., whether <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is enough to determine the behavior of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) and <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conciseness</em> (i.e., how easy it is to grasp <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Soft Determinacy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an explanation <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> of an <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> on a database <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> “sufficiently determines” <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> behaves similarly even if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> changes, as long as the result of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is not affected by this change.
Ideally, for any two databases that agree on the explanation views in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> should be identical. Viewing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a query, this strict sufficiency notion is precisely <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">determinacy</em>, as described in <a href="#S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" title="Determinacy ‣ 2.1. Relational Database Concepts ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. To capture the imperfect nature of explanations more effectively, we relax the notion of determinacy in two dimensions. First, we do not require exact determinacy;
instead, we account for <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximate agreement</em> by quantifying the distance between model predictions before and after the change
(see <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Second, we move away from deterministic determinacy by providing a probabilistic adaptation. For that, we require determinacy <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in expectation</em>, using our original database <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> as reference and assuming a probability distribution of <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contingency databases</em>.
Applying the two relaxations leads to an approximate probabilistic notion of determinacy, namely <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft determinacy</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, we assume a distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> over contingency databases that agree on the schema <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
To be comparable to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, we require each database <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this distribution to agree with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on the keys of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, that is:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]=D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]=D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to define approximate agreement, suppose that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> maps an instance <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, and an instance <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> ito <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use a distance metric <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">dist</span> that gives a quantity <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{dist}(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{dist}(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of how far <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is from <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, consider the special case where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary classifier with predictions <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>; then, it makes sense to use <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> that checks for identity:
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{dist}(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{dist}(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is
0 if <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
1 otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using the above, we arrive at the property of soft determinacy for an explanation <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, quantified by <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deviation from determinacy</em>, so that lower deviation implies a better explanation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 2 (Deviation from Determinacy).</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For an instance <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and a distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of contingency databases,
the (instance-specific) deviation from determinacy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E,s)</annotation></semantics></math> of an explanation <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">expected distance</em> between the prediction for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and the prediction on a contingency <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, given that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> respects the views:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E,s)\vcentcolon=\underset{D^{\prime}\sim\Delta}{\mathbb{E}}\Big[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">D</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></munder><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E,s)\vcentcolon=\underset{D^{\prime}\sim\Delta}{\mathbb{E}}\Big[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textrm{dist}(M_{D}(s),M_{D^{\prime}}(s))\;\Big|\bigwedge_{V\in E}V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D)=V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D^{\prime})\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">dist</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋀</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{dist}(M_{D}(s),M_{D^{\prime}}(s))\;\Big|\bigwedge_{V\in E}V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D)=V\mathord{\langle s\rangle}(D^{\prime})\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deviation from determinacy of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is then the average over all instances:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)\vcentcolon=\frac{1}{|D(R)|}\sum_{s\in D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]}\hskip-11.38109pt\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E,s)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)\vcentcolon=\frac{1}{|D(R)|}\sum_{s\in D(R)[\mathsf{key}(R)]}\hskip-11.38109pt\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E,s).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Explanation Conciseness</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We associate with each <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> a <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cost</em> related to its complexity.
This cost can be syntactic, e.g., the number of symbols involved in the SQL representation.
Alternatively, it can be semantic, e.g., the number of tuples in the view <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(D)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(D)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote this cost by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define the cost of an explanation <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(E)=\sum_{V\in E}\mathsf{cost}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(E)=\sum_{V\in E}\mathsf{cost}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the syntactic cost does not depend on the database <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, as the SQL representation refers only to the schema <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The computational problem of finding the best explanation can be phrased as an optimization problem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 3 (Soft determinacy explanations).</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-model <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and a database <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the soft determinacy explanation problem asks for a solution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subseteq\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subseteq\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> to the following optimization objective:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{E}[\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)+\lambda\cdot\mathsf{cost}(E)]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>E</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{E}[\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)+\lambda\cdot\mathsf{cost}(E)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some trade-off captured by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{R},\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{R},\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly, there is a natural trade-off between achieving a high level of determinacy (i.e., the minimization of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>) and low explanation complexity (the minimization of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>). Consider the extreme case of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> containing the entire schema <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> fully determines the model, but also has the complexity of the model that we wish to explain to begin with. On the other hand, a completely uninformative explanation <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> has minimal complexity, but is likely to hide what the model requires for its predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The computational objective involves the estimation of a conditional probability, a generally intractable task, in order to compute <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#S4" title="4. Instantiating the Framework for Selected SQL Fragments ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we discuss several choices for realizing this objective into a tractable optimization problem. Additionally, in <a href="#S5" title="5. GNN-Specific Approach ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we propose a learning-based solution for efficient explanation view discovery in the case of GNNs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Instantiating the Framework for Selected SQL Fragments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework presented in <a href="#S3" title="3. RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is abstract and requires the choice of an explanation language <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> together with a concrete metric for conciseness and the distribution of contingency databases <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this section, we give specific instantiations that we deem useful and general. In the next sections, we propose heuristic implementations of these over GNN models and test them in experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Explanation Languages</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our explanation framework highlights the database components that provide the most useful information for the model. These may include important features, important feature combinations, or influential values and value ranges of particular features. In SQL terms, these explanations can be expressed through three core operations: projecting only the relevant attributes of relations, joining relations to reveal informative combinations, and selecting tuples that satisfy specific conditions. To capture these explanations, we focus on three simple SQL fragments utilizing the fundamental relational algebra operations: projections, joins, and selections. Throughout the remainder of this paper, we restrict our attention to instance-agnostic explanation views, for which we define suitable choices of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> and later propose efficient algorithms. We leave the study on instance-specific explanations for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To define each <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> precisely, we classify the attributes of a relation schema <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in a database schema <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> into three categories. Given <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})=(A_{1},\dots,A_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(\mathcal{R})=(A_{1},\dots,A_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, an attribute <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">key attribute</em> if it appears in the key signature <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">foreign-key attribute</em> if it appears on the left-hand side of a foreign-key constraint in <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FK}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FK}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. Any attribute that is neither a key nor a foreign key is called a <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data attribute</em>. In the definitions of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> that follow, these three attribute types are treated separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(1) The language <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> can be used to highlight subsets of data attributes corresponding to important features, while always preserving all key attributes and foreign-key attributes. Formally, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> contains views of the form:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex13.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">select</span><span id="S4.Ex13.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S4.Ex13.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Ex13.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Ex13.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\dots,A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\dots,A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.Ex13.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which project onto a chosen subset of data attributes <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\dots,A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\dots,A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, while retaining the sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> of key attributes and the sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> of foreign-key attributes of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(2) The language <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span> consists of foreign-key joins. Each explanation view in <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span> is associated with a foreign-key constraint <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊑</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]\sqsubseteq\mathsf{key}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> in the schema, and has the form:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex14.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">select</span><span id="S4.Ex14.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S4.Ex14.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.Ex14.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Ex14.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Ex14.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Ex14.m1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Ex14.m1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]=s[\mathsf{key}(S)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r[\mathord{\mathbf{C}}]=s[\mathsf{key}(S)]</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">These views retain all tuples produced by joining <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> using foreign-key references. Note that more complex join patterns can be captured by multiple views in an explanation view set. Because <span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span> focuses on foreign-key attributes, it always preserves all key attributes as well as the data attributes of relations that appear in at least one explanation view.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(3) The language <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> focuses on filtering tuples based on attribute values. As with <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, the emphasis is on data attributes, while key and foreign-key attributes are always preserved. Formally, <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> contains views of the form:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex15.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">select</span><span id="S4.Ex15.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S4.Ex15.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.Ex15.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Ex15.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a selection predicate that identifies an informative subset of tuples. We assume that <span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> does not affect the graph topology, hence we implicitly assume that an explanation in <span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> includes all views of the form “<span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">select <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span>” for every <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{key}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{key}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> contains all key attributes and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains all foreign-key attributes of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For example, consider a database with a relation <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Product</span> <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The explanation view</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex16.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif">select</span><span id="S4.Ex16.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_sansserif"> <math id="S4.Ex16.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.Ex16.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Ex16.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P.price&lt;50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P.price&lt;50</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Ex16.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P.cat=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P.cat=</annotation></semantics></math> “electronics”</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">indicates that low-priced electronics constitute an informative region of the data for the model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost model</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Explanation conciseness requires a precise definition of the cost model so that every explanation view <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in E</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a value <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we propose a simple cost model that focuses on minimizing the description cost of explanation views, making them easier for a user to understand. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of highlighted data attributes, i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)=k</annotation></semantics></math>. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>, each binary join incurs a cost of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the complexity of the selection predicate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Later, we will consider disjunctions of the form <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif"> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an atomic predicate. In this case we simply assign <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{cost}(V)=l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗌𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{cost}(V)=l</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the number of atomic predicates involved.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Language composition</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider combinations of the three languages by constructing composite explanation views in the relational algebra sense. Importantly, these combination languages produce composite views rather than unions of separate explanation views belonging to one of the three defined languages. For composite views, the cost model is naturally extended: we sum the number of data attributes in projections, the number of joins, and the number of selection predicates involved.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Database Perturbations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the definition of deviation from determinacy (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2 (Deviation from Determinacy). ‣ 3.2. Soft Determinacy ‣ 3. RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) allows the liberty to choose any distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of contingency databases, as long as they agree with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on the explanation views <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider two extreme scenarios to illustrate the impact of the choice of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. First, consider a <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> over databases <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that differ from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in just one value of a single tuple.
A well-behaved model would likely give a similar output on <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, irrespective of the chosen explanation <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, such <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> would not yield a discriminative criterion between candidate explanations. On the other hand, suppose <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution over all possible <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, no matter how different they are from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, it is not clear how one could obtain samples from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and, more importantly, the sampled <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> could be unrepresentative for the application domain of the original database <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Ideally, we would like to limit the explanatory power of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to the useful regime, i.e., restricting <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> to <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a local neighborhood</em> of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that captures realistic variations while preserving relevance to the application domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each explanation language <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math>, we give a number of strategies for realizing <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> by perturbing the original database <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting databases <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are substantially different from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, yet maintain certain properties of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving a balance in the aforementioned tradeoff.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core idea is to permute the values of columns that are not used in any projection explanation, inspired by a well-known model-agnostic feature selection technique,
namely <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">permutation feature importance</em> (PFI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Breiman, 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fisher et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Molnar, 2025</a>)</cite>.
Such column-wise permutations respect the active domain of each attribute and preserve aggregate statistics within a column, while breaking correlations between a permuted column and the model output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Example 1.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The example in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In (1) Projection ‣ 4.2. Database Perturbations ‣ 4. Instantiating the Framework for Selected SQL Fragments ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows two column-wise permutation strategies.
The attributes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are part of the explanation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> are not.
Thus, we permute <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> to produce different <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can either apply the permutations to both attributes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> jointly (left) or independently (right).
Note that the latter completely breaks correlations between <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly creating unnatural tuples in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this example, we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=2\cdot C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=2\cdot C</annotation></semantics></math> for all tuples in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Joint permutation preserves this property in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Attr}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Attr}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of attributes used in views of projection explanations <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Attr}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Attr}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> includes all key attributes. Because explanation views must be respected by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we can only permute attributes not present in projection explanations. For each relation name <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{attPerm}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍𝖯𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{attPerm}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of attributes in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{att}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{att}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> not in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Attr}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Attr}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>, which are allowed to be permuted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. A relation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">column-wise permutation</em> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> under <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> explanation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, written <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}\in\mathsf{perm}_{E}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝗉𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}\in\mathsf{perm}_{E}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same schema and cardinality as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each tuple <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a unique tuple <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in r</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}[\mathsf{key}(R)]=t[\mathsf{key}(R)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}[\mathsf{key}(R)]=t[\mathsf{key}(R)]</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathsf{attPerm}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍𝖯𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathsf{attPerm}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}[A]</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation of the multiset of values of the attribute <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, while for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\notin\mathsf{attPerm}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍𝖯𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\notin\mathsf{attPerm}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}[A]=t[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}[A]=t[A]</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint column-wise permutation</em> <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, written <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}\in\mathsf{jointperm}_{E}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍𝗉𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}\in\mathsf{jointperm}_{E}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined similarly, but all attributes in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{attPerm}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍𝖯𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{attPerm}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> are permuted by the same permutation, preserving <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}[\mathsf{key}(R)]=t[\mathsf{key}(R)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}[\mathsf{key}(R)]=t[\mathsf{key}(R)]</annotation></semantics></math> for the unique corresponding <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The column-wise permutation distribution <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution with support: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{D^{\prime}\mid\forall R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S}):D^{\prime}(R)\in\mathsf{perm}_{E}(D(R))\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝗉𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D^{\prime}\mid\forall R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S}):D^{\prime}(R)\in\mathsf{perm}_{E}(D(R))\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The joint column-wise permutation distribution <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution with support: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{D^{\prime}\mid\forall R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S}):D^{\prime}(R)\in\mathsf{jointperm}_{E}(D(R))\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍𝗉𝖾𝗋𝗆</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D^{\prime}\mid\forall R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S}):D^{\prime}(R)\in\mathsf{jointperm}_{E}(D(R))\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#S6" title="6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> we conduct experiments with both <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and show that the deviation from determinacy is robust to this choice.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09482v1/DB_Perturbations_1.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:197/77;" width="197" height="77"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09482v1/DB_Perturbations_2.svg" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:197/77;" width="197" height="77"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example permutations for <span id="S4.F2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> (upper) and <span id="S4.F2.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> (lower).
For <span id="S4.F2.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, only attributes <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (in orange) are in <math id="S4.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Attr}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Attr}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>
of explanation <math id="S4.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <span id="S4.F2.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span>, the tuples (in orange) <math id="S4.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(1,0,1,2),(2,1,2,4)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(1,0,1,2),(2,1,2,4)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="S4.F2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Tups}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝗎𝗉𝗌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Tups}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. The key attribute <math id="S4.F2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is always retained.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>, the goal of a perturbation is to make joins uninformative unless they appear in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the joins we consider always correspond foreign-key constraints, we apply a perturbation on the foreign key side. This perturbs the target join without affecting other joins on the side of the primary key.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Example 2.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The example in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In (2) FKJoin ‣ 4.2. Database Perturbations ‣ 4. Instantiating the Framework for Selected SQL Fragments ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (left) shows perturbations applied to the primary-foreign pair <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}.B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}.B</annotation></semantics></math> - <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}.B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}.B</annotation></semantics></math>.
One could consider permuting the foreign key <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}.B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}.B</annotation></semantics></math> as in a projection explanation. But this strategy would not be effective in breaking the predictive signal encoded in the actual join condition as it would retain the number of primary-foreign key pairs per unique value of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}.B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}.B</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> would appear 4 times while <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> would each appear once. In order to break the join information, we randomly replace each value of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}.B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}.B</annotation></semantics></math> with a value in the domain <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>, either uniformly at random (right) or mimicking the underlying frequency distribution of foreign keys (middle).
In the former each value in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math> appears <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> times while in the latter <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> appears 4 times while <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> each appear once, preserving the frequencies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4,1,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4,1,1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FK}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FK}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of foreign keys that appear in the explanation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a perturbed relation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by independently replacing the values of foreign-key attributes outside of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FK}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FK}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> with other values from their respective domains. We consider two alternative strategies for generating <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform replacement</em> <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{uniform}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗎𝗇𝗂𝖿𝗈𝗋𝗆</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{uniform}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, each foreign key value is replaced independently and uniformly at random from its domain. In the <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frequency-preserving replacement</em> <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{freq}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝖿𝗋𝖾𝗊</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{freq}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, the multiset of foreign key values in the perturbed relation matches the multiset of frequencies in the original relation: if a foreign key value occurs <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> times in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists some foreign key value in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that occurs <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> times, although not necessarily in the same tuples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09482v1/DB_Perturbations_3.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:246/109;" width="246" height="109"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Example 2. ‣ (2) FKJoin ‣ 4.2. Database Perturbations ‣ 4. Instantiating the Framework for Selected SQL Fragments ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Example</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: foreign key perturbations for <span id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>.
Here, the foreign key <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}.B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}.B</annotation></semantics></math> is perturbed as it is is not in <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FK}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FK}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> we adapt the column-wise permutation strategies used in the case of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>. Here, we permute <em id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> data attributes only for the tuples that do not satisfy the selection condition of the explanation view. We provide an example in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In (1) Projection ‣ 4.2. Database Perturbations ‣ 4. Instantiating the Framework for Selected SQL Fragments ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (lower).
Specifically, let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Tups}(E)\subseteq\bigcup\{D(R)|R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖳𝗎𝗉𝗌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊆</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝗅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Tups}(E)\subseteq\bigcup\{D(R)|R\in\mathsf{rel}(\mathcal{S})\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of tuples that satisfy the selection condition. Each relation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=D(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is separated into <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{E}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{E}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{E}\subseteq\mathsf{Tups}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝗎𝗉𝗌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{E}\subseteq\mathsf{Tups}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{E}^{\prime}=r\setminus r_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{E}^{\prime}=r\setminus r_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then apply column-wise permutations, either independently or jointly, only in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{E}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{E}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Attr}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Attr}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> including all data attributes, i.e., only preserving key and foreign key attributes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>GNN-Specific Approach</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we focus on models that are based on hetero-GNNs, as defined in <a href="#S2" title="2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and propose a heuristic approach to solving the optimization in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3 (Soft determinacy explanations). ‣ 3.3. Explanation Conciseness ‣ 3. RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
One approach to face this challenge is to exhaustively search over the space of candidate explanation views and estimate the deviation from determinacy of each candidate.
However, such direct optimization is intractable, as the space of possible views is prohibitively large.
As an alternative, we leverage the fact that deep learning libraries are naturally equipped with gradient-based continuous optimization;
we use this optimization to efficiently discover locally optimal explanation views via a surrogate <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continuous</em> optimization problem.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Mask Learning: From Discrete to Continuous Optimization</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formulate a continuous optimization problem, we adopt <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mask learning</em>, a well-established technique for model interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fong and Vedaldi, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ying et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Mask learning introduces real-valued <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learnable masks</em> that can then be mapped to candidate explanations in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math>, as we will see shortly.
In general, mask learning leverages the fact that deep learning models operate with internal feature representations. <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Masking</em> means selectively hiding some features while retaining others, ideally keeping only the most important ones.<span id="footnote1a" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Notice that we use the term masking in the common ML “selector” convention
where higher mask values reveal more of the original signal.
</span></span></span>
Concretely, let <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a feature vector.
Applying a <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mask component</em> <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> means replacing <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a new feature vector as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathord{\mathbf{x}},m)=m\cdot\mathord{\mathbf{x}}+(1-m)\cdot\mathord{\mathbf{u}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathord{\mathbf{x}},m)=m\cdot\mathord{\mathbf{x}}+(1-m)\cdot\mathord{\mathbf{u}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S5.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">replacement vector</em> of the same dimension as <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this replaces <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, if <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> is uninformative (e.g., the zero vector), masking removes the influence of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Multiple mask components can be applied in the model simultaneously on different feature vectors <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The collection of these mask components is a <em id="S5.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mask</em> <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any value of the mask <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can obtain a model output, enabling mask components to be learned via gradient descent with an appropriate loss function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Language-Specific Masking</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
