math id="S5.F4.pic1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.54 -18.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.57em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="7.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.86)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F4.pic1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RDL model pipeline with hetero-GNNs. The figure shows where different masks are located in the pipeline.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Depending on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math>, masking is applied at the feature encoding stage or the message-passing stage of the model, as depicted in <a href="#S5.F4" title="In 5.2. Language-Specific Masking ‣ 5. GNN-Specific Approach ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For projection explanations, we apply mask components attribute-wise in the feature encoding space. Since projection explanations necessarily include all primary and foreign keys, we restrict masks to non-key attributes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each relation <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assign one mask component <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{R.A_{i}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{R.A_{i}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> per attribute <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathsf{att}(R)\setminus\big(\mathsf{key}(R)\cup\mathsf{FK}(R)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖺𝗍𝗍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗄𝖾𝗒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>𝖥𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathsf{att}(R)\setminus\big(\mathsf{key}(R)\cup\mathsf{FK}(R)\big)</annotation></semantics></math>. For each node <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, the same mask vector <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{R.A_{1}},\ldots,m_{R.A_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{R.A_{1}},\ldots,m_{R.A_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is applied at feature encoding, changing <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.4. Relational Learning via Heterogeneous Graphs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}=\mathsf{Enc}_{R}(&amp;\mu(\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{1}}(t[A_{1}]),m_{R.A_{1}})\oplus\cdots\oplus\\
&amp;\mu(\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}(t[A_{k}]),m_{R.A_{k}}))\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Enc</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}=\mathsf{Enc}_{R}(&amp;\mu(\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{1}}(t[A_{1}]),m_{R.A_{1}})\oplus\cdots\oplus\\
&amp;\mu(\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}(t[A_{k}]),m_{R.A_{k}}))\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathord{\mathbf{x}},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathord{\mathbf{x}},m)</annotation></semantics></math> applies the mask component <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to the encoding vector <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When masking attribute <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use a replacement vector <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{u}}=\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{i}}(t^{\prime}[A_{i}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{u}}=\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{i}}(t^{\prime}[A_{i}])</annotation></semantics></math> for a uniformly and randomly sampled tuple <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures the replacement of the attribute’s encoding is uninformative, yet realistic. Masks for all relations and attributes are learned jointly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After mask learning, continuous mask values are mapped into projection explanations. For that, we use a threshold <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{R.A_{i}}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }m_{R.A_{i}}\geq\delta\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{R.A_{i}}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }m_{R.A_{i}}\geq\delta\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Attributes with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{R.A_{i}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{R.A_{i}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> are included in the projection explanations; the rest are omitted.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For join explanations, we apply masks during message-passing. Every primary/foreign-key pair corresponds to an edge type in the graph (see <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Relational Learning via Heterogeneous Graphs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>). Thus, we learn a mask value <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\lambda_{i}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\lambda_{i}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for every edge type <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to decide whether to retain the corresponding join in the explanation. <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3. Heterogeneous Graph Neural Networks ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is adapted as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}(v)=\mathsf{UPD}(\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell-1)}(v)\,,\,&amp;\mu(\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{1},o_{1}),m_{\lambda_{1}}),\dots,\\
&amp;\mu(\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{q},o_{q}),m_{\lambda_{q}}))\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖴𝖯𝖣</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell)}(v)=\mathsf{UPD}(\mathord{\mathbf{h}}^{(\ell-1)}(v)\,,\,&amp;\mu(\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{1},o_{1}),m_{\lambda_{1}}),\dots,\\
&amp;\mu(\mathcal{A}^{(\ell-1)}(v,\lambda_{q},o_{q}),m_{\lambda_{q}}))\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that for each edge type <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the same mask component <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\lambda_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\lambda_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied at the aggregate vector of messages irrespective of the direction <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, as both directions correspond to the same join. Also, the same mask components are used across all layers <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, because message-passing corresponds to the same join operation irrespective of the GNN layer.
We replace each aggregate with a zero replacement vector <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly to <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, continuous mask values are mapped to join explanations after thresholding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, consider the case of a single selection predicate <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. We assign a mask <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\phi}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\phi}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to all tuples that satisfy <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. For each node <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{(R,t)}=m_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{(R,t)}=m_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Masking is then applied per tuple by adapting <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.4. Relational Learning via Heterogeneous Graphs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> into:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}=\mu(\mathsf{Enc}_{R}\left(\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{1}}(t[A_{1}])\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}(t[A_{k}])\right),m_{(R,t)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤𝗇𝖼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Enc</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}=\mu(\mathsf{Enc}_{R}\left(\mathsf{Enc}_{R.A_{1}}(t[A_{1}])\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathrm{Enc}_{R.A_{k}}(t[A_{k}])\right),m_{(R,t)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly to <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, when masking <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(R,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(R,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{u}}=\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{u}}=\mathord{\mathbf{X}}_{v^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a randomly sampled <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}=(R,t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}=(R,t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the implementation of <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span>, we consider conditions that involve disjunctions of atomic predicates <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}\vee\dots\vee\phi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}\vee\dots\vee\phi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following real-valued logic techniques that map logical formulas into differentiable form <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, we employ the Łukasiewicz t-conorm for disjunctions. Thus, the mask values per tuple are calculated as
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{(R,t)}=\min\!\left\{1,\sum_{\phi_{i}(t,\mathord{\mathbf{c}}_{i})=1}m_{\phi_{i}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{(R,t)}=\min\!\left\{1,\sum_{\phi_{i}(t,\mathord{\mathbf{c}}_{i})=1}m_{\phi_{i}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A similar strategy may be applied for conjunctions. After learning masks for all candidate selection predicates jointly, we apply thresholding to map them into discrete explanations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To combine selections and projections in a composite language, namely <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">ProjSelect</span>, we first learn projection masks as discussed for <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, and then learn masks for candidate selection predicates that use data attributes present in the learned projections. In our experiments (<a href="#S6" title="6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we restrict ourselves to categorical attributes with equality predicates and numerical attributes with range predicates defined via quantiles.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Mask Optimization</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the loss function for mask learning, a surrogate optimization for our original problem in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3 (Soft determinacy explanations). ‣ 3.3. Explanation Conciseness ‣ 3. RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\mathord{\mathbf{m}}}\ \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\lambda\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}(\mathord{\mathbf{m}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝐦</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.667em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\mathord{\mathbf{m}}}\ \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}+\lambda\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}(\mathord{\mathbf{m}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the mask <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector that contains all mask components to be learned, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original loss of the model (e.g., classification or regression loss) and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-regularization term applied to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathord{\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathord{\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the size of the mask.
Note that <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the learned masks preserve the model’s behavior, a goal that is aligned with the soft determinacy objective of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3 (Soft determinacy explanations). ‣ 3.3. Explanation Conciseness ‣ 3. RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. On the other hand, the <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> penalty encourages sparsity in the masks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hastie et al., [n.d.]</a>)</cite>, promoting concise explanations by selecting only the most relevant database components.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Experimental Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess the performance of our proposed method and the overall effectiveness of the framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main metric we consider is deviation from determinacy <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2 (Deviation from Determinacy). ‣ 3.2. Soft Determinacy ‣ 3. RDL Explanations via Soft Determinacy ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The distance <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">dist</span> is the absolute difference for binary classification and the normalized absolute difference <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{dist}(x,y)=\frac{|x-y|}{|x|+|y|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{dist}(x,y)=\frac{|x-y|}{|x|+|y|}</annotation></semantics></math> for regression, both bounded in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> estimate is averaged over 5 perturbation samples; we report both mean and standard deviation. Second, we evaluate the explanation size <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that this is the number of projected data attributes for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>, the number of join conditions for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>, and the number of predicates for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span>. Finally, we report the time to generate the explanation, which corresponds to training time for mask-based methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use RelBench, the standard benchmark suite for RDL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robinson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which includes real-world relational databases and diverse predictive tasks. We focus on node-level tasks, i.e., regression and binary classification.
Details of the datasets are provided in the online appendix. As <a href="#S7.T2" title="In Other GNN explanations ‣ 7. Related Work ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows, we denote each combination of database <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and task <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> as D<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>T<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models to explain</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train RDL models from scratch.
For each database, we construct a graph representation (as explained in <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Relational Learning via Heterogeneous Graphs ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) and use PyTorch Frame <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to implement feature encoders.
The GNN is implemented in PyG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fey et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, with 32-dimensional encoders. We perform hyperparameter tuning over the learning rate, batch size, and number of GNN layers.
Models are trained using the train/validation/test split provided by the benchmark. Our models reach performance similar to the one previously reported <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robinson et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for GNN approaches.
For each explanation task, we sample 100 instances for mask training and 100 instances for explanation evaluation. Since many classification tasks are imbalanced, we use balanced sampling to ensure that the selected instances contain an equal number from each class. For temporal tasks we perform temporal sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fey et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to ensure time-consistency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Methods</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mask-based:
We implement the learnable masks as introduced in <a href="#S5" title="5. GNN-Specific Approach ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, with early stopping.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="20.2" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.09px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 20.2 20.2" width="20.2"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,20.2) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.1,0) translate(0,10.1)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 9.83 0 C 9.83 5.43 5.43 9.83 0 9.83 C -5.43 9.83 -9.83 5.43 -9.83 0 C -9.83 -5.43 -5.43 -9.83 0 -9.83 C 5.43 -9.83 9.83 -5.43 9.83 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.4 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="12.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1a</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Column Mask</span>: masks for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>. <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="21.17" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.26px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 21.17 21.17" width="21.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,21.17) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.58,0) translate(0,10.58)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 10.31 0 C 10.31 5.69 5.69 10.31 0 10.31 C -5.69 10.31 -10.31 5.69 -10.31 0 C -10.31 -5.69 -5.69 -10.31 0 -10.31 C 5.69 -10.31 10.31 -5.69 10.31 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.76 -4.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="13.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1b</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FKPK Mask</span>: masks for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>. <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic4" class="ltx_picture" height="19.7" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-5.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 19.7 19.7" width="19.7"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,19.7) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(9.85,0) translate(0,9.85)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 9.57 0 C 9.57 5.29 5.29 9.57 0 9.57 C -5.29 9.57 -9.57 5.29 -9.57 0 C -9.57 -5.29 -5.29 -9.57 0 -9.57 C 5.29 -9.57 9.57 -5.29 9.57 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.04 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.94em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="12.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.pic4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1c</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Filter Mask</span>: masks for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since no prior work generates SQL explanations for RDL, we implement a set of baselines.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> Ranking-based for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>:
We implement two baselines that order data attributes according to their importance, and select the top-<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for each target explanation size <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. A known limitation of such methods is that they ignore dependencies between attributes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Molnar, 2025</a>)</cite>.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="20.2" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.09px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 20.2 20.2" width="20.2"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,20.2) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.1,0) translate(0,10.1)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 9.83 0 C 9.83 5.43 5.43 9.83 0 9.83 C -5.43 9.83 -9.83 5.43 -9.83 0 C -9.83 -5.43 -5.43 -9.83 0 -9.83 C 5.43 -9.83 9.83 -5.43 9.83 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.4 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="12.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2a</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Local Impact</span>: The importance of a data attribute is computed as <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> when the attribute is taken as an explanation by itself. The lower the <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> the more important the attribute. <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="21.17" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.26px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 21.17 21.17" width="21.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,21.17) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.58,0) translate(0,10.58)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 10.31 0 C 10.31 5.69 5.69 10.31 0 10.31 C -5.69 10.31 -10.31 5.69 -10.31 0 C -10.31 -5.69 -5.69 -10.31 0 -10.31 C 5.69 -10.31 10.31 -5.69 10.31 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.76 -4.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="13.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2b</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PFI</span>: We adapt Permutation Feature Importance (PFI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Molnar, 2025</a>)</cite> by measuring the change in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> when each attribute is removed. Attributes are ranked by impact, where higher deviation implies higher importance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Greedy approach for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> and <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>:
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="20.2" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.09px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 20.2 20.2" width="20.2"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,20.2) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.1,0) translate(0,10.1)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 9.83 0 C 9.83 5.43 5.43 9.83 0 9.83 C -5.43 9.83 -9.83 5.43 -9.83 0 C -9.83 -5.43 -5.43 -9.83 0 -9.83 C 5.43 -9.83 9.83 -5.43 9.83 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.4 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="12.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3a</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Greedy</span>: Starting from the empty set, we iteratively add the attribute that yields the largest reduction in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="21.17" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.26px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 21.17 21.17" width="21.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,21.17) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.58,0) translate(0,10.58)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 10.31 0 C 10.31 5.69 5.69 10.31 0 10.31 C -5.69 10.31 -10.31 5.69 -10.31 0 C -10.31 -5.69 -5.69 -10.31 0 -10.31 C 5.69 -10.31 10.31 -5.69 10.31 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.76 -4.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="13.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3b</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Greedy Expansion</span>: At each step, we add a join that maximally reduces <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> while ensuring connectivity to the prediction entity. This is more computationally feasible than <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Greedy</span>due to fewer join candidates at each iteration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">5</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Random Subset</span> for <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>: We randomly sample the data attributes and report the average over 5 samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">4</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Empty</span>:
An explanation containing no information, resulting in a full database perturbation. We use this as a point of reference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation details</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the embeddings of textual attributes we use a BERT-based model<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/distilbert-base-nli-mean-tokens" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/distilbert-base-nli-mean-tokens</a></span></span></span>.
All experiments are conducted on a VM with an RTX 6000 Ada GPU, 14 vCPUs, and 188 GB of RAM. Our code is available at <a href="https://github.com/agapiR/rdl-explain" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/agapiR/rdl-explain</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation plan</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the proposed mask learning strategy separately for the <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> and <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span> languages. Our goal is to evaluate the tradeoff between conciseness and soft determinacy. To ensure a fair comparison, we use the same explanation size for all methods, denoted as <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we determine by thresholding the learned mask as discussed in <a href="#S5" title="5. GNN-Specific Approach ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
We also assess the robustness of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> under different database perturbation strategies <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Additionally, we conduct a case study on one task where we showcase <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Selection</span> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> composite languages.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span><span id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.F5" title="In 6.1. Projection Evaluation ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the results for <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> across all datasets and tasks, including averages (AVG).<a href="#S6.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 6.1. Projection Evaluation ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 6.1. Projection Evaluation ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> show <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for two different perturbation strategies. The main takeaway is that <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Column Mask</span> produces higher quality explanations (in terms of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>) than the baselines on most tasks, and on average, while outperforming them on execution time (<a href="#S6.F5.sf3" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 6.1. Projection Evaluation ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a>) by 1-2 orders of magnitude for large databases. This is expected since <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Column Mask</span>’s running time is dependent on the size of the model and the size of the explanation task, which is usually small.
Among the baselines, <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PFI</span> gives the best results both on quality and on time.
However, all baselines suffer from their costly dependence on the schema size, i.e., the number of data attributes that they consider.
On database D<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PFI</span> is faster, but this is due to the fact that the database is small and has a concise schema.
Comparing the two perturbation strategies, they appear to yield very similar results,
indicating that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> is robust to the choice of permutation strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While <a href="#S6.F5" title="In 6.1. Projection Evaluation ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> assumes the same explanation size <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S6.F6" title="In 6.1. Projection Evaluation ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows
<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for various explanation sizes <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, illustrating the tradeoff between the two.
As the size <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases, we generally observe <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dev_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dev_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> decreasing, i.e., the explanation quality increasing.
This is not true for <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Random Subset</span>, which shows that the choice of attributes in the explanation is crucial.
For smaller <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the baselines are often better than <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Column Mask</span>, but <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Column Mask</span> usually outperforms them for the optimal <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the end of the axis).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09482v1/time_comparison_legend_column.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/29;" width="476" height="29"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09482v1/dev_from_determinacy_combined_column_permutation_independent.png" id="S6.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/65;" width="476" height="65" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Deviation from determinacy <math id="S6.F5.sf1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for each database-task and database perturbation <math id="S6.F5.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09482v1/dev_from_determinacy_combined_column_permutation_joint.png" id="S6.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/65;" width="476" height="65" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Deviation from determinacy <math id="S6.F5.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for each database-task and database perturbation <math id="S6.F5.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09482v1/time_comparison_column_permutation_independent.svg" id="S6.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/67;" width="476" height="67"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S6.F5.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Time to obtain explanation for each database-task and <math id="S6.F5.sf3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗂𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{ind}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. Results for <math id="S6.F5.sf3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\mathsf{joint}}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> are similar.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S6.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation for <span id="S6.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>. For visualization purposes, tasks are separated into “easy” (left) and “hard” (right) ones, with separate <math id="S6.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> scales. The average (AVG) across all tasks is also shown. Below the dataset and task name, we indicate the explanation size <math id="S6.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the total number of data attributes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
