</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.T1" title="In 6.3. Case Study ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates how simple explanation views can yield low <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖽𝖾𝗏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{dev}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, below 0.1 for compact languages such as <span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span> and <span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span>. The results also highlight the flexibility offered by the <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> choice. By combining projections, joins, and selections, one can balance size, data reduction, and expressivity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.SS3.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<figure id="LSTx1" class="ltx_float ltx_lstlisting">
<div id="LSTx1.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FCF4DC;--ltx-border-color:#91A0C1;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,U0VMRUNUICBpZCwgbmN0X2lkLAogICAgICAgIGFsbG9jYXRpb24sIGludGVydmVudGlvbl9tb2RlbCwgcHJpbWFyeV9wdXJwb3NlCkZST00gICAgZGVzaWducyBkCldIRVJFICAgZC5hbGxvY2F0aW9uPTAgIE9SICBkLmludGVydmVudGlvbl9tb2RlbD0w" download="">⬇</a></div>
