</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<figure id="LSTx2" class="ltx_float ltx_lstlisting">
<div id="LSTx2.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#91A0C1;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,U0VMRUNUICogRlJPTSBzdHVkaWVzIHMKSk9JTiAgZmFjaWxpdGllc19zdHVkaWVzIGZzICBPTiAgcy5uY3RfaWQ9ZnMubmN0X2lkCkpPSU4gIGZhY2lsaXRpZXMgZiAgT04gIGZzLmZhY2lsaXR5X2lkPWYuZmFjaWxpdHlfaWQ=" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx5" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">*</span><span id="lstnumberx5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">studies</span><span id="lstnumberx5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">s</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx6" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">JOIN</span><span id="lstnumberx6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">  </span><span id="lstnumberx6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">facilities_studies</span><span id="lstnumberx6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">fs</span><span id="lstnumberx6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">  </span><span id="lstnumberx6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">ON</span><span id="lstnumberx6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">  </span><span id="lstnumberx6.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">s</span><span id="lstnumberx6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx6.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">nct_id</span><span id="lstnumberx6.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">=</span><span id="lstnumberx6.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">fs</span><span id="lstnumberx6.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx6.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">nct_id</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx7" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">JOIN</span><span id="lstnumberx7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">  </span><span id="lstnumberx7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">facilities</span><span id="lstnumberx7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">f</span><span id="lstnumberx7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">  </span><span id="lstnumberx7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">ON</span><span id="lstnumberx7.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">  </span><span id="lstnumberx7.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">fs</span><span id="lstnumberx7.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx7.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">facility_id</span><span id="lstnumberx7.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">=</span><span id="lstnumberx7.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">f</span><span id="lstnumberx7.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx7.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">facility_id</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"></figcaption>
</figure>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F8.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example explanation view using <span id="S6.F8.sf2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also qualitatively examine the selection predicates learned by our <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Filter Mask</span> approach. Our method detects the following predictive selection predicates: <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">designs.allocation = 0</span> selects single-arm trials, <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">designs.intervention_model = 0</span> selects trials applying an intervention to a single group and the predicate <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">studies.phase <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0,2,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0,2,4\}</annotation></semantics></math></span> selects trials based on the phase. <a href="#S6.F8.sf1" title="In 6.3. Case Study ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F8.sf2" title="Figure 8(b) ‣ 6.3. Case Study ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a> show two example explanation views. The table <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">designs</span> originally contains <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> attributes, of which we maintain only <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> data attributes, together with the key and foreign-key attributes. In <a href="#S6.F8.sf1" title="In 6.3. Case Study ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a>, we apply the disjunction of two selection predicates which together select the study designs that correspond to single-arm clinical trials or single-group intervention trials. We can infer that these trials might be simpler, cheaper or faster to conduct. The view indicates that this knowledge about the study’s design is highly predictive of its outcome.
In <a href="#S6.F8.sf2" title="In 6.3. Case Study ‣ 6. Experimental Evaluation ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a>, the view captures an important join path of length 2. (The two joins correspond to two separate views in our language <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span>, but they are shown in a single view here.)
It indicates that the (potentially multiple) facilities where a study is conducted are important for its outcome.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4. </span>Retraining Results</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a sanity check, we retrain the models using only the subset of the data retained by our <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Projection</span> and <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">FKJoin</span> explanations. As <a href="#S7.T2" title="In Other GNN explanations ‣ 7. Related Work ‣ Database Views as Explanations for Relational Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows, performance is comparable for most tasks while the database size is substantially smaller. This confirms that the selected data retain the core predictive signal.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning over a relational database</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical relational learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Getoor and Taskar, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raedt et al., 2016</a>)</cite> is one of the first approaches to directly leverage the relational structure, factorized learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schleich et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olteanu, 2020</a>)</cite> avoids costly joins, automatic feature engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kanter and Veeramachaneni, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chepurko et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stanoev et al., 2024</a>)</cite> synthesizes new features, while other approaches train arbitrary (often tree-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Guestrin, 2016</a>)</cite>) predictive models.
The currently emerging approach is Relational Deep Learning (RDL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cvitkovic, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fey et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. It models the relational database as a heterogeneous graph, and employs tabular feature encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> trained jointly with Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) to solve a wide range of predictive tasks.
The current research frontier further builds specialized architectures for RDL that fall under the same GNN paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hilprecht et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and adapts Graph Transformers to the relational setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rampášek et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dwivedi et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Of course, the RDL approach inherits the opacity of “black-box” deep learning, creating the need for explainability. Since RDL models, irrespective of their implementation, make predictions at the database level, we consider explanations that refer to the database rather than the
specific modeling components, such as the heterogeneous graph.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ML explanations</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the explainability in RDL, we review the broader literature on ML explanations. These are categorized by their perspective as either <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local</em> or <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">global</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guidotti et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samek and Müller, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Molnar, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bassan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Global approaches aim to explain a model’s overall behavior, while local approaches focus on individual predictions. Approaches also vary in focus: some rely on producing local approximations using simpler models (e.g., LIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, SHAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee, 2017</a>)</cite>), while others highlight <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">critical input subsets</em>, such as the rule-based conditions generated by Anchors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. A more formal distinction further classifies the latter into <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">abductive</em> and <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contrastive</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marques-Silva and Ignatiev, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barceló et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Abductive explanations identify minimal subsets of the input <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sufficient</em> to preserve the model’s prediction, whereas contrastive explanations specify the minimal changes required to alter it. In our context, explanation views are global, as they refer to the database as a whole rather than particular instances, and abductive, as they identify the database components sufficient to determine the model’s behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Subgraph-based GNN explanations</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Instance-level</em> explanations specific to Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) aim to answer questions of the type “Why does the GNN produce a particular output <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">for a given instance</em> (i.e., node in the graph)?”.
Most of these explanations are at the level of individual graph elements, i.e., the explanation is a subgraph of the input graph.
Some instance-level explainers focus on neighboring nodes and their individual features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pope et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vu and Thai, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Funke et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, others on important edges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ying et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lucic et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, while others on connected subgraphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mika et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Few are tailored to hetero-GNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mika et al., 2023</a>)</cite> by specializing subgraph explanations to meta-paths.
Similarly, <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">global</em> GNN explainers, which provide insights for a GNN across all instances, use subgraphs as explanations.
More specifically, some recover a collection of subgraphs that collectively explain many instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv and Chen, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Shen, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> aiming at explanation generality, while others extract instance-level explanation subgraphs which are then combined into global insights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azzolin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In contrast to our approach, most global explainers implicitly assume the predictive tasks are motif-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azzolin et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> and they are often evaluated against a ground truth. We refer the reader to a recent survey for more details on these techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. We depart from all these subgraph-based approaches in the following ways:
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> Our explanations are expressed in terms of the relational database, which is the input object, instead of the graph that is internally constructed by the model.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> The complexity of our explanations is measured in terms of the complexity of SQL views, which can succinctly refer to billions of data points.
This implies that subgraph-based explanations can be expressed as SQL views, often much more succinctly than in graph-based representations.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> Our SQL explanations can express database components that go beyond simple subgraphs.
For example, a single-column projection does not correspond to a connected subgraph. It is also possible to provide explanations that correspond to derived information, such as aggregates which do not explicitly exist in the graph at all.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other GNN explanations</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond subgraphs, there are other types of explanations that are fundamentally different. First, some approaches, if adapted to RDL, would not correspond to any database elements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vu and Thai, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baldassarre and Azizpour, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schnake et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwarzenberg et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlichtkrull et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, e.g., because they refer to GNN layers or construct surrogate models. Second, distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pluska et al., 2024</a>)</cite> can map the GNN into a logical classifier. This is similar in spirit to our explanations; however, their base predicates refer to inner features of the model and can quickly become too complex. Finally, self-explainable GNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai and Wang, 2021</a>)</cite> claim to provide simpler GNN models, thus with better interpretability properties. Recently this approach has been adapted to RDL, but it is limited to meta-path selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ferrini et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Our explanations are given post-hoc on an already trained model, instead of training a simpler model from scratch.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T2" class="ltx_table">
