<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Success metrics for explanations</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most prior work focuses on sufficiency of explanations, i.e., finding a small subgraph that (by itself) retains the GNN prediction, quantified by a metric called <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fidelity</em><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Fidelity is sometimes referred to as fidelity minus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amara et al., 2022</a>)</cite> or sufficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Longa et al., 2025</a>)</cite></span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Variants of this metric either focus on retaining prediction confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> or robustness under random, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, infinitesimally small <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and even adversarial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> perturbations.
In addition to sufficiency, some works assess explanation necessity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amara et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Longa et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, i.e., whether the explanation subgraph is essential to maintain the GNN prediction.
Closest to soft determinacy is a fidelity variant called degree of sufficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azzolin et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azzolin et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> in that it has a probabilistic nature.
Compared to all this prior work, our evaluation has the following key differences:
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>  Since the framework is general, soft determinacy is parameterized by the explanation language <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast to subgraph modifications, the perturbations needed for soft determinacy calculation are defined in the database space and must respect <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{EL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{EL}</annotation></semantics></math> to be considered valid.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>  We minimize explanation complexity with respect to query instead of data size.
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="16.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.77px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 16.46 16.46" width="16.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(8.23,0) translate(0,8.23)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.95 0 C 7.95 4.39 4.39 7.95 0 7.95 C -4.39 7.95 -7.95 4.39 -7.95 0 C -7.95 -4.39 -4.39 -7.95 0 -7.95 C 4.39 -7.95 7.95 -4.39 7.95 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>  We also focus on the time to obtain an explanation, which has received limited attention in prior work, with few exceptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Explanations in databases</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of databases, explainability research targets various phenomena including why specific query results appear or are absent (why-not explanations) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glavic et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chapman and Jagadish, 2009</a>)</cite>, outliers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miao et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Madden, 2013</a>)</cite>, trends in query outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roy and Suciu, 2014</a>)</cite>, and anomalies related to performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kalmegh et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. These efforts parallel ML explainability in aiming to explain algorithmic outputs via input data and/or computational logic. Explanations in databases are often expressed in terms of individual tuples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meliou et al., 2010a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meliou et al., 2010b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salimi et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Livshits et al., 2021</a>)</cite> or query provenance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Buneman et al., 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suciu et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Green and Tannen, 2017</a>)</cite>.
Differently, in our framework, explanations are themselves simple queries, explaining complex queries (deep models).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Conclusions and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented a framework for abductive global explanations of machine-learning models over relational databases. Grounded in a probabilistic adaptation of determinacy, our approach enables interpretable and flexible explanations as relational queries of varying fragments, without depending on model internals. We studied the instantiation of the framework on an architecture based on heterogeneous GNNs, and introduced an efficient masking-based heuristic that circumvents the need to compute soft determinacy directly. Our extensive evaluation on the RelBench benchmark
demonstrated that our framework achieves high predictive accuracy with low runtime cost across diverse tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several directions for future work emerge from our framework. A first class of extensions concerns broadening the applicability and expressive power. One natural direction is to develop efficient instantiations for additional model classes, such as attention-based models that gain traction in relational learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dwivedi et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. It is also important to develop solutions for other tasks, such as link prediction.
Another direction is to enrich the explanation languages by supporting instance-specific explanations and incorporating constructs such as group-aggregate queries and more complex selection conditions. This raises important design questions, such as how to systematically select good candidate predicates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these, a fundamental question is how to identify explanation languages that are naturally aligned with a given use case—can we predict which explanation constructs will yield the most useful results for a particular combination of model architecture and database schema? For instance, it may turn out that group-aggregate explanations are especially well-suited to GNNs, as both rely on aggregation of related tuples (e.g., grouping by the same foreign keys). Formalizing and testing such alignments could enable systems that not only allow flexible adaptation to explanation needs, but also guide users toward proper explanation languages.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
